    "description": "Seymour's Second Neighborhood Conjecture states that every finite oriented graph (a digraph with no loops and no digons) has a vertex whose second out-neighborhood is at least as large as its first out-neighborhood. The minimum-out-degree at most 1 case is fully settled: a minimum-out-degree vertex of degree 0 is a sink, and one of degree 1 has an out-neighbor which itself has an out-neighbor landing in the second neighborhood. The first genuinely open regime begins at minimum out-degree 2. Proposed approach: analyze a minimal counterexample, show it is 2-out-regular near a minimum vertex, and use a discharging / local weighting argument on the union of the closed out-neighborhoods of a minimum-degree vertex and its two out-neighbors to force a Seymour vertex.",
